# Line endings. Everything in this repo is text that is read by POSIX tools as
# often as by Windows ones, so the index AND the working tree are LF.
#
# Why eol=lf and not the default `text=auto` alone: `text=auto` normalises the
# index to LF but still checks out CRLF on Windows. That is exactly how the
# shipped hooks break - a Windows clone writes `#!/usr/bin/env sh\r\n`, the file
# is copied/installed onto a Linux box (install.sh, a container, CI) and the
# kernel reports `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env sh^M`. Same class of breakage as
# the missing +x bit, arriving by a different route. Forcing eol=lf also silences
# the `LF will be replaced by CRLF` warning on every write from Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out for the files where a CRLF is an actual bug, not a nuisance:
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
# .md: not executable, but these are skill payloads that get concatenated,
# diffed and fed to models; mixed endings make diffs and hashes noisy for no
# gain, and no Windows tool we use needs CRLF markdown.
*.md   text eol=lf
# .ps1: PowerShell (5.1 and 7) parses LF-only scripts fine, including
# here-strings, so LF is safe. It is chosen for consistency, not necessity -
# one rule for the whole tree beats a per-extension exception nobody remembers.
*.ps1  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# The one genuine exception: cmd.exe is unreliable with LF-only batch files
# (labels and multi-line blocks can misparse). If we ever ship one, it is CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never touch these.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
